"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nIn , the author employs his imagination and scientific knowledge to explore the very nature of the —pure hard sci-fi.  \nThis series features a lot of talk about dimensions.  \nI tried to visualize everything at first, but eventually, I gave up trying to imagine it all.  \n\nThe same human greed and arrogance seen in the second book reappears in this one.  \n\nI really didn't like the female protagonist. She's too idealistic and naive.  \nShe couldn't distinguish between ideals and reality, and the consequences were disastrous.\n\nPersonally, I felt the story could have ended with The Dark Forest.  \nHowever, I suppose this volume was necessary for a truly complete conclusion."